 ## Background
-The Distance-Adjusted Eikonal Loss was introduced in the context of neural rendering methods that use Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) to represent surfaces. It was proposed to address the limitations of standard Eikonal regularization, which tends to negatively impact background reconstruction [S1]. The method builds upon the VolSDF approach, which parameterizes the object surface as an SDF to facilitate surface extraction and provide analytical normals [S2]. The specific distance-adjusted weighting scheme was detailed in HybridNeRF [S1], while the broader context of SDF representation and Eikonal loss in radiance field inversion was presented in [S2].
+The Distance-Adjusted Eikonal Loss was introduced in the context of neural rendering methods that use Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) to represent surfaces [S1, S2]. It was proposed to address the limitations of standard Eikonal regularization, which tends to negatively impact background reconstruction [S1]. The method builds upon the VolSDF approach, which parameterizes the object surface as an SDF to facilitate surface extraction and provide analytical normals [S2]. The specific distance-adjusted weighting scheme was detailed in HybridNeRF [S1], while the broader context of SDF representation and Eikonal loss in radiance field inversion was presented in [S2].
 
